===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One of the main [[section]]s into which a published work is divided, especially a [[book]].
#: {{ux|en|Detective novel writers try to keep up the suspense until the last '''chapter'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|VIII|passage=At her invitation he outlined for her the succeeding '''chapters''' with terse military accuracy ; and what she liked best and best understood was avoidance of that false modesty which condescends, turning technicality into pabulum.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:Nintendo Power|date=December 2003|volume=174|page=33|title=Fire Emblem|section=Strategy with a Story|text=The story that unfolds through 70 '''chapters''' featuring intense tactical battles is full of plot twists and turns.|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nintendo-power-issue-174-december-2003_202301/page/33/mode/1up}}<!--video game-->
## A section of a work, a collection of works, or fragments of works, often manuscripts or transcriptions, created by scholars or advocates, not the original authors, to aid in finding portions of the texts.
##* {{quote-book|en|title=A Church Dictionary| author=Walter Farquahar Hook| year=1842| year_published=1896| passage='''CHAPTER'''&nbsp;- One of the principal divisions of a book, and, in reference to the Bible, one of the larger sections into which its books are divided. This division, as well as that consisting of verses, was introduced to facilitate reference, and not to indicate any natural or accurate division of the subjects treated in the books.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Twelfth-century Decretal Collections and Their Importance in English History| author=Charles Duggan| year=1963| page=127| passage=At least thirty-two of the first forty-seven decretal '''chapters''' were received by English ecclesiastics,}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1983| title=Revue théologique de Louvain&nbsp;- Volume 14| page=127| passage=il conclut: « No pope, no collection but the masters who served the one and commented on the other ultimately determined the content of this decretal '''chapter'''».}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Walter Map and the Matter of Britain| page=67| author=Joshua Byron Smith| year=2017| passage=But unlike a similar annotation, it has not been turned into a '''chapter''' title because the annotation “de quodam hermita” (Of a certain hermit) immediately preceded it. If a scribe had also turned ''Liberauit Dominus hermitam'' into a '''chapter''' title, he would have created a '''chapter''' that consisted of a single sentence, which even for a rather dull scribe would seem unacceptable. {{...}} While scribe A wrote the body of the text of the De nugis curialium, scribe X contributed the rubricated '''chapter''' headings.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=New Studies in the Manuscript Tradition of Njáls saga| page=xvii|author=Svanhildur Óskarsdóttir; Emily Lethbridge| year=2018| passage=While rubrics do not appear in paper manuscripts, eight paper manuscripts contain '''chapter''' titles, which serve the same function as the medieval rubrics.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=A Textual History of Cicero's Academici Libri| page=152| author=David J. Hunt| year=2018| passage=Characteristics: parchment; 92 fols.; 260 × 180 mm.; 32 lines; Italy; ca. 1430; two hands: humanistic rotunda with some Gothic features; marginal notes by first hand and names by a second hand; space left for initials; rubric inscriptions; superscript variants by first hand; '''chapters''' marked in margin; horizontal catchwords; quaternions.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=The Unheard Voice of Law in Bartolomé de Las Casas’s Brevísima Relación de la Destruición de las Indias| page=97| author=David T. Orique| year=2021| passage=From canon law, he cited Gratian's ''Decretum'', Gregory's ''Decretales'', the Sexto and Clementines ''Decretales'', and the ''Extravagantes'', and specifically referred to “the rules for understanding the Law” in a decretal '''chapter''' on ''Propterea''.}}
# Certain ecclesiastical bodies (under [[canon law]])
## An assembly of monks, prebendaries and/or other clergymen connected with a cathedral, conventual or collegiate church, or of a diocese, usually presided over by the dean.
## A community of [[canon]]s or [[canoness]]es.
## A [[bishop]]'s [[council]].
# A section of a social body.
## An administrative division of an [[organization]], usually [[local]] to a specific [[area]].
## An organized branch of some society or fraternity, such as the Freemasons.
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1862|journal=The Freemasons' Monthly Magazine
|passage=If the By-Law which admits honorary members is silent upon their rights, they may perhaps be determined by a consideration of which of these classes was intended by the '''Chapter''' in admitting them}}
# A [[meeting]] of a chapter of certain organized societies or orders.
# A [[chapter house]]<ref>{{R:Burrill Law Dictionary}}</ref>
# A sequence (of events), especially when presumed related and likely to continue.
#* '''1866''', {{w|Wilkie Collins}}, ''[[s:Armadale/Book the Last/Chapter I|Armadale]]'', Book the Last, Chapter I,
#*: "You know that Mr. Armadale is alive," pursued the doctor, "and you know that he is coming back to England. Why do you continue to wear your widow's dress?" &para; She answered him without an instant's hesitation, steadily going on with her work. &para; "Because I am of a sanguine disposition, like you. I mean to trust to the '''chapter''' of accidents to the very last. Mr. Armadale may die yet, on his way home."
#* {{RQ:Stoker Worm|chapter=26|passage={{...}}she determined to go on slowly towards Castra Regis, and trust to the '''chapter''' of accidents to pick up the trail again.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1958 June|title=The Last British Atlantic|journal=Railway Magazine|page=383|text=A '''chapter''' of locomotive history was closed on Sunday, April 13, when the last Atlantic tender locomotive to remain in service on British Railways, No. 32424, ''Beachy Head'', ended its working life of more than 46 years.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 February 8|author=Tony Streeter|title=Kirkdale: home to Merseyrail's new '777s'|journal=RAIL|issue=976|page=36|text=With the entry into service of the '777s', a new '''chapter''' opens in the long history of Merseyside's electric suburban railwsys.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[location]] or [[compartment]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|I|v|passage=In his bosom! In what '''chapter''' of his bosom?}}
# {{lb|en|Roman Catholicism}} A [[prescribe]]d [[reading]] at one of the [[canonical hour]]s.
#: {{syn|en|capitule}}

